-- Version 6 → 7: an admin decides who is allowed agent access.
--
-- One column on `users`, and the interesting thing about it is what it does NOT
-- do: it does not back-fill. See Users.sq's mcp_allowed for what the column
-- means and why it must not share one with mcp_enabled.
--
-- ── The back-fill that is deliberately absent ───────────────────────────────
--
-- DEFAULT 0 means every account that already exists lands on "not permitted".
-- That is the whole intent — a permission that assumed itself for everyone who
-- happened to sign up before it existed would be no permission at all, and the
-- deployment where that matters is precisely the one that has been running for
-- months.
--
-- The consequence is real and was accepted rather than overlooked: at the deploy
-- that runs this, every connected agent stops working. Not "stops being
-- authorized at its next token refresh" — stops at its next request, because
-- /mcp re-reads this per request (McpServer.resolveMcpUser). Nothing is deleted;
-- the tokens and grants survive untouched, so an admin opening the instance
-- settings dialog and granting access restores every agent with no reconnection
-- and no second trip through the browser. That asymmetry is exactly why this is
-- a gate and not a purge — see Users.sq.
--
-- The admin themselves is not exempt. There is no `WHERE is_admin = 1` here, and
-- adding one is the tempting change: it would spare whoever deploys this from
-- locking out their own agent. It is still wrong. Admin-ness short-circuits
-- AccessControl's rules about projects; it has never exempted anybody from the
-- mcp_enabled gate, and a permission with a silent exception is one nobody can
-- reason about from the column alone.
--
-- SUPERSEDED, and left standing because a migration is history rather than
-- doctrine: the statement about this migration remains true — it still back-fills
-- nothing, and it still lands every existing account on 0 — but the conclusion no
-- longer holds. UserRecord.isMcpPermitted now reads `mcp_allowed || is_admin`, so
-- an admin left on 0 by this migration is permitted anyway and never needed the
-- `WHERE is_admin = 1` this paragraph talks anybody out of. See Users.sq.
--
-- RENAMED SINCE, and the name above is deliberately left alone for the same
-- "history rather than doctrine" reason: 11.sqm renames this column to
-- `is_sys_admin`, to tell the instance-wide administrator apart from the
-- per-project one that arrived with it. At version 6 the column really was
-- `is_admin`, so that is what this file says; read every `is_admin` above as
-- today's `is_sys_admin`. Nothing else about the paragraph changes.
--
-- ── Why ALTER TABLE suffices ────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- No REFERENCES clause and a constant non-null default, which is the case
-- SQLite's ALTER TABLE handles directly. 2.sqm had to rebuild `issues` because
-- its column was NOT NULL and composite-keyed; there is nothing like that here.
-- Rebuilding anyway would be strictly worse — a rebuild runs with foreign keys
-- off (see Database.migrateSchema), and a DROP TABLE users with the constraints
-- off would take every issue, comment and grant in the database with it.
--
-- NOT NULL with DEFAULT 0 is legal in ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN precisely because
-- the default is a constant: SQLite can fill every existing row without reading
-- one. A non-constant default would be rejected.

ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN mcp_allowed INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;
